m the Medieval University to the Catholic Institute: The Catholic Institute of Toulouse (ICT) is the distant heir of the university founded in the 13th century, April 12, 1229, in application of the Treaty of Peace signed between Louis IX, King of France, and Raymond VIl, Count of Toulouse , under the auspices of the Cardinal Legate of Pope Gregory IX. The seal of the Catholic Institute, "whose engraving dates back to the 13th century, is almost certainly the main seal of the University at its beginnings.  The Université catholique de Toulouse opened on November 15, 1877 with a Faculty of Law.  Following the law of March 18, 1880, which prohibits private higher institutions to wear the title of University, that of Toulouse favors the name of Institut catholique de Toulouse, which it has kept to this day.") (More history of the Catholic Institute may be found at the following websites.)uhttp://www.ict-toulouse.fr/en/ict.htmluhttp://www.ict-toulouse.fr/fr/l-ict/historique.html01766cz  a2200325n  n  81081466 DLC20180824073318.0810811n| azannaabn          |n aaa        an  81081466   a(Uk)000698846  a(OCoLC)oca00626719  aDLCbengerdacDLCdDLCdUkdOCoLC  f1945-09-052edtf1 aRussell, Norman,d1945-  dDr  aChristianityaFathers of the church2lcsh  aOratory of St. Philip Neri (London, England)2naf  aSt. Stephen's House (Oxford, England)  aClergy2lcsh  aMales2lcsh  aeng  qNorman V.  aHistoria monachorum in Aegypto. English. The lives of the desert fathers, 1981, c1980 (a.e.)bt.p. (Norman Russell) jkt. (priest, member of the London Oratory)  aCyril of Alexandria, 2000:bCIP t.p. (Norman Russell) data sheet (b. 5 Sept. 1945) pub. info. (former memb. of the London Oratory)  aSt. Stephen's House, Oxford website, viewed 17 August 2018b(Dr Norman Russell is an Honorary Research Fellow at St Stephen's House; from 1972 to 1987 he was a member of the London Oratory; since leaving the Oratory he has pursued a career as an independent scholar and translator; he is the author of several books on patristic theology, including Cyril of Alexandria, The doctrine of deification in the Greek patristic tradition, Theophilus of Alexandria & Fellow workers with God; in 2010 he joined the Orthodox Church; Norman V. Russell) 0aCyril of Alexandriaf2000 4aThe doctrine of deification in the Greek patristic traditionf2004 0aTheophilus of Alexandriaf2007 0aFellow workers with Godf200902615cz  a2200433n  n  81081860 DLC20180822073554.0811012n| azannaabn          |b aaa        an  81081860 7 aQ40698512wikidata7 ahttps://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q40698512uri  a(OCoLC)oca00627110  aDLCbengerdacDLCdDLCdIENdCSt-HCdNNCdHU  f1900-10-15g1980-11-162edtf1 aAronson, Boris,d1900-1980  aKiev (Ukraine)bNew York (N.Y.)cUkrainecUnited StateseNew York (N.Y.)2naf  aTheaters--Stage-setting and sceneryaCostume design2lcsh  aSet designersaCostume designers2lcdgt1 aAharonson, Barukh,d1900-19801 aAranson, Boris,d1900-19801 aArnson, B.,d1900-19801 aAronson, B.,d1900-19801 aAronson, Baruch,d1900-19801 aAronson, Barukh,d1900-19801 aAronson, Boris Solomonovich,d1900-19801 aАронсон, Борис Соломонович,d1900-19801 aאראנסאן, ב.,d1900-19801 aארנסאן, ב.,d1900-1980  aNon-Latin script references not evaluated.  aGeorge, W. Boris Aronson et l'art du ... 1928.  aBoris Aronson--from his theatre work, c1981:bt.p. (Boris Aronson) p. 3 (d. Nov. 16, 1980)  aBiog. resource center online, June 5, 2002b(Arnoson, Boris (1900-1980); also known as: Boris Solomon Aronson; source: DAB, supplement 10, 1976-1980)  aAmer. nat. biog. online, June 5, 2002b(Aronson, Boris ( Oct. 1900-16 Nov. 1980); scene and costume designer; b. Kiev)  a20th century day-by-day Web site, Nov. 16b(Boris Aronson; b. Oct. 15, 1900; d. Nov. 16, 1980)  aSocial security death index, June 5, 2002b(Aronson, Boris; b. Oct. 15, 1899; d. Nov. 1980)  aJew Suss, 1929bDLC copy theater program