at the UCLA Cotsen Institute of Archaeology)  aViking language 2, 2015bback cover (Jesse Byock teaches Old Norse, Icelandic sagas, and Viking archaeology. He received his PhD from Harvard Univ. and is Distinguished Prof. of Old Norse Studies in the UCLA Scandinavian Section and Professor at the UCLA Cotsen Institute of Archaelogy. Prof. Byock directs the Mosfell Archaeolgical Project (MAP) in Iceland. His books include Viking Age Iceland, Feud in the Icelandic Saga, The Prose Edda: Norse Mythology, Grettir's Saga, and The Saga of the Volsungs: The Norse Epic of Sigurd the Volsung)01712cz  a2200325n  n  82066757 DLC20180824073320.0820628n| azannaabn          |a aaa        an  82066757   a(OCoLC)oca00763592  aDLCbengerdacDLCdDLCdOCoLCdDLCdICU  f1920-09-20g2003-11-062edtf 0aPG5038.Z551 aZink, Lubor Jan,d1920-  aKlápy (Czech Republic)  bOttawa (Ont.)2naf  cEngland2naf  cCanadas1958t20032naf  aJournalists2lcsh  aMales2lcdgt  aczeaeng1 aZink, Lubomír,d1920-1 aZink, Lubor,d1920-1 aZink, Lubor J.q(Lubor Jan),d1920-0 aŘíz,d1920-2003  aHis Dva roky, 1941.  aHis What price freedom? 1981:bt.p. (Lubor J. Zink)  aWikipedia, the free encyclopedia, October 13, 2016:bLubor J. Zink page (Lubor J. Zink; born September 20, 1920 in Klapý, Czechoslovakia; died November 6, 2003; Czech-Canadian writer and columnist known for his anti-Communism; fought in the exile Czech army during World War II; fled Czechoslovakia in 1948; became British subject in 1949; moved to Canada in 1958; became Canadian citizen in 1963; worked as Ottawa-based columnist for the Toronto Telegram and, later, the Toronto Sun)uhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lubor_J._Zink  aiForum WWW site, August 23, 2018:bLubor Zink [20.9.1920 Klápy - 6.11.2003, Ottawa] page, dated 12 November 2014 (Lubor Zink; Lubomir Zink; journalist and poet; went into exile in 1948; died in Ottawa on 6 Novmeber 2003)uhttps://iforum.cuni.cz/IFORUM-15430.html01731c