to Italy, she made films there and in England, Germany, Mexico and Spain; after returning to American films, she married Donald Burton in 1982 and resided in Hampstead, London in the 1980s; they remained together until Burton's death from emphysema in their home in Cathedral City, California in 2007)02973cz  a2200421n  n  83160247 DLC20180822073556.0830915n| azannaabn          |a aaa        an  83160247   a(OCoLC)oca00984531  aDLCbengerdacDLCdMiEMdDLCdIEN  f1827g1897-02-152edtf  f1847?vErlikh (1982)0 aAlula,cRas,d1827-1897  cEthiopia2naf  aEthiopia--History--19th centuryaEthiopia--Politics and government--19th centuryaEthiopia--History, Military2lcsh  aPoliticiansaSoldiersaGenerals2lcsh  aMales2lcdgt0 aAbba Nega ,d1827-18970 wnnaaAlula,cRas,d1847?-18970 aʾAlulā ʾabā nagā,cRās,d1827-18970 aʾĀlulā ʾEngedā,crāʾesi,d1827-18970 aAlula Engeda,cRas,d1827-18970 aAlula Engeda Qubi,cRas,d1827-18970 wnneaAlula Qubi,d1847?-18970 aGabra-Mikāʾēl,d1827-18971 aQubi, Alula,d1827-18970 aRās Alulā,d1827-1897  aErlikh, Ḥ. Ethiopia and Eritrea during the scramble for Africa, c1982 (subj.)bt.p. (Rās Alulā) p. 5 (d. 2/15/1897; father's name: Engdā Qubi) p. 7 (b. 1847?) p. 8 (baptismal name: Gabra-Mikāʾēl)  aEncyc. Africana dic. of Afr. biog., c1977:bv. 1, p. 49 (Alula Qubi (1827-1897))  aBahru, History of modern Ethiopia, 1991b(Alula Engeda, Ras)  aProuty and Rosenfeld, Historical dictionary of Ethiopia and Eritrea, 1994b(Alula Engeda Qubi, Ras (c. 1847-1897))  aMāmo Wedenah, ʾAlulā ʾabā nagā, 1986.  aMuluwarq Kidānamāryām, Mawāʾel, 1995:bt.p. (rāʾesi ʾĀlulā ʾEngedā (ʾĀbā Nagā))  aErlikh, H. Ras Alula and the scramble for Africa, 1996:bp. 5 (d. 15 Feb. 1897; son of Engdā Qubi) p. 7 (b. 1847?) p. 216 (Rās Alulā is son of Engda, who is son of Qubi)  aWikipedia, August 20, 2018b(Ras Alula; born 1827 near Abiy Addi, Tembien, Ethiopia; also known by his horse name Abba Nega and by Alula Qubi; from peasant origins; demonstrated his military skill in Battle of Gundet and Gurs, 1875 and 1876, and several other major battles, including Dogali and Adwa; promoted to Ras in 1876 by Emper Yohannes IV; wounded in battle 15 January 1897, died a month later from his wounds; considered by Ethiopians to be the best military mind that the country has ever produced)  aHistorical dictionary of Ethiopia, 2013, viewed online August 20, 2018b(Alula Qubi, Ras (1827-1897); trusted general of Yohannes IV, governor of Mareb Mellash, and chief architect of military resistance to Egyptian and Italian encrouchment in Ethiopia; born in Marerraivé, a village about 15 miles south of Abbi-Addi, capital of Tigray's Tamben District; began his career as a nagadras, or chief customs officer)01752cz  a2200325n  n  83160843 DLC20180825073520.0831109n| azannaabn          |a ana        an  83160843 zn  84149910 zn  84149911   a(OCoLC)oca00984952  aDLCbengerdacDLCdTxU-MudDLCdOCU-MudTxHUdNcU-Mu2 aFrankfurter Museumsorchester2 wnneaFrankfurter Oper.bOrchester2 aFrankfurter Opernorchester2 aFrankfurt Opera Orchestra2 aOrchester der Frankfurter Oper2 aFrankfurter Opernhaus- und Museumsorchester2 aFrankfurter Museumorchester2 aFrankfurter Museums-Orchester2 aFrankfurter Opern- und Museumsorchester2 aStädtisches Opernhaus- und Museumsorchester  aWeill, K. Die Dreigroschenoper [SR] 1978?:blabel (Orchester der Frankfurter Oper) container (Frankfurter Opernorchester; Frankfurt Opera Orchestra)  aMahler, G. Sinfonie Nr. 8 Es-Dur [SR] p1981:blabel (Frankfurter Opernhaus- und Museumsorchester)  aWozzeck [VR] 2003?:bcontainer (Frankfurter Museumorchester)  aGlanert, D. Caligula [SR] 2010, p2006:bcontainer (Frankfurter Opern- und Museumsorchester)  aOper Frankfurt WWW site, Nov. 27, 2004b(Frankfurter Museumsorchester; orchestra of Oper Frankfurt, est. 1808)  aOper Frankfurt WWW site, June 23, 2010b(Fra