1946)  aInvitation to a wedding, 2010:bt.p. (Natalie Kononenko) added t.p. (Наталія Кононенко)  aEtiquette and taboos around the world, 2017:bpage 371 (Natalie Kononenko is professor and Kule Chair in Ukranian Ethnography at the University of Alberta)  aUniversity of Alberta, Faculty of Arts web site, August 16, 2018:b(Natalie Kononenko, PhD, MA, BA, Professor & Kule Chair in Ukrainian Ethnography, Arts, Modern Languages and Cultural Studies ; natalie.kononenko@ualberta.ca, nataliek@ualberta.ca ; I was born in a displaced persons camp in Germany in 1946.  I came to the United States in 1951 and my family had only $100 which was hidden in the head of my doll.  I grew up in New Jersey and attended University at Cornell, moving on to Radcliffe College and then on to Harvard University for graduate work.  I received my PhD from Harvard University in Slavic and Near Eastern Languages, Literatures, and Folklore ; taught Russian Language and Slavic Folklore,) CV (Department of Modern Languages and Cultural Studies, University of Alberta ; Home: Edmonton, Alberta ; Education: Ph.D., granted jointly by Slavic Languages and Literatures and Near Eastern Languages and Literatures, Harvard University, 1976; M.A., Department of Slavic Languages and Literatures, Harvard University, 1969; B.A., Department of Slavic Languages and Literatures, Radcliffe College, 1967; Also attended Cornell University 1963-65 ; Academic Employment: University of Alberta, 2004-present; University of Virginia, 1975-2004)uhttps://www.ualberta.ca/arts/about/people-collection/natalie-kononenko00541cz  a2200169n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003100115100002000146372001600166374002900182670007800211670008200289n  97029554 DLC20180825073536.0970320n| azannaabn          |n aaa        an  97029554   a(OCoLC)oca04299678  aDLCbengerdacDLCdNcU-Mu1 aFrisani, Rosita  aMusic2lcsh  aSopranos (Singers)2lcsh  aDorindo, dormi ancor? 199