which he graduated in 1923. 1925 pursued his studies in Agriculture and later in Education at North Texas University. It was during these years he nurtured his passion for poetry, and continued to write poems in English. For a brief period after the death of his son he wrote poetry in Arabic, but admitted the English language opened wider horizons for self-expression)  aJawdat Haydar website, August 2, 2016b(Jawdat R. Haydar (1905-2006) biography (born 23 April 1905 in Baalbek, Lebanon;  1923, graduated from the Syrian Protestant College, now the American University of Beirut; 1927-1928, graduated with a B.S. in Education from North Texas State University; 1928-1930, appointed principal of the National College of Alieh in Lebanon; 1930-1931, appointed director of Al-Najah College in Nablus, Palestine and Member of the Board of Higher Education; 1932-1960, joined the International Petroleum Company (IPC) of Iraq, rising to the position of Industrial Relations Advisor; 1960, Resigned from IPC and returned to his hometown Baalbek, where he settled, dedicating the rest of his years to writing poetry. Haydar passed away on 4 December 2006)  aCenter for Educational Research and Development website, August 2, 2016:beducational magazine > Jawdat Haydar's biography (Jawdat R. Haydar; in 1960, he returned to Baalbeck where he had a dream to spend his life in writing poetry. He wrote four anthologies in English published by Vantage Press, New York)01071cz  a2200193n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003300115110002500148370002900173410009600202410003700298670018200335670012300517670023700640no2005056088DLC20180822073635.0050616n| azannaabn          |a ana     c  ano2005056088  a(OCoLC)oca06658987  aInUbengerdacInUdDLCdInU2 aIndiana Nongame Fund  eIndianapolis (Ind.)2naf1 aIndiana.bDivision of Fish and Wildlife.bWildlife Diversity Section.bIndiana Nongame Fund2 aIndiana Endangered Wildlife Fund  