rk (N.Y.)2naf  aUniversities and colleges--AdministrationaArts--ManagementaBroadcasting2lcsh  aColumbia University. School of the Arts2nafs1966t1975  aColumbia Broadcasting System, inc.2naf  aExecutivesaBroadcastersaDeans (Education)aArts administrators2lcsh  aComposers' Forum concert, 1958 November 8, 1958:bconcert program (Davidson Taylor, moderator)  aNew York Times, July 28, 1979, viewed online, August 13, 2018bpage 22 (Davidson Taylor; born Shelbyville, Tennesee, February 26, 1907; died July 27, 1979, New York City; 1933, he became the announcer for CBS Carnegie Hall concerts of the New York Philharmonic under Arturo Toscanini; 1938, director of music for CBS, commissioning works by Aaron Copland, William Schumann, Samuel Barber and other American composers; Post World War II he was named Vice President of CBS and put in charge of programming; he was later named director of public affairs; 1951-1958, worked for NBC, attaining the position of vice president; he was instrumental in expanding the network's radio and television coverage of science, music and the arts; he served as a consultant to the New York Public LIbrary, helping to plan the Lincoln Center Library-Museum of the Performing Arts; at various times, he was a member of arts advisory committees for a number of educational and cultural organizations for the State of New York; 1959, became an administrator for the Columbia University's art-center program; helped in founding the Columbia University School of the Arts in 1966, and served as its first director and dean; he retired from Columbia in 1975.01849cz  a2200301n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040004000115046001500155100002900170370003100199372005100230373010400281374002000385374002600405375001700431377000800448378001800456400002500474670024100499670012700740670022300867670028001090670017701370no2018107407DLC20180822073526.0180813n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  ano2