ca11506766  aWaUbengerdacWaU0 aInachusc(Greek deity)  cGreek deity  cRiver god  cGods, Greek2lcsh  cWater gods2lcsh  cGreece2naf  fArgos (Greece)2naf  aMales2lcdgt0 aInachosc(Greek deity)0 aInakhosc(Greek deity)0 áΙναχοςc(Greek deity)0 aІнахc(Greek deity)0 aInakhc(Greek deity)0 aИнахc(Greek deity)0 aÍnacc(Greek deity)0 aÍnacoc(Greek deity)0 aInaĥoc(Greek deity)0 aInakoc(Greek deity)0 aInachasc(Greek deity)0 aイーナコスc(Greek deity)0 aĪnakosuc(Greek deity)0 aInahc(Greek deity)  aNon-Latin script references not evaluated.  aSutton, Dana Ferrin. Sophocles' Inachus, 1979.  aWikipedia, August 17, 2018:bInachus (In Greek mythology, Inăchus, Inachos or Inakhos (Ancient Greek: ́Ιναχος = Inachos) was the first king of Argos; for modern scholars, Inachus is the most ancient god or hero of Argos; most modern mythologists understand Inachus as one of the river gods, all sons of Titans Oceanus and Tethys) Belarusian page (Інах = Inakh) Bulgarian page (Инах = Inakh) Breton page (Inac'hos) Catalan page (Ínac) Welsh page (Inachus) German page (Inachos) Estonian page (Inachos) Spanish page (Ínaco) Esperanto page (Inaĥo) Basque page (Inako) French page (Inachos or Inachus) Italian page (Inaco) Lithuanian page (Inachas) Dutch page (Inachus) Japanese page (イーナコス = Īnakosu) Russian page (Инах = Inakh) Serbian page (Инах = Inah) Finnish page (Inakhos) Ukrainian page (Інах = Inakh)  aTheoi Project website, August 17, 2018b(Inakhos; Greek name: Ιναχος = Inachos; Latin spelling: Inachus. River-God of Argolis in the Peloponnesos, southern Greece. When the gods Poseidon and Hera contested for dominion over Argos, Inakhos was appointed arbiter and decided in Hera's favour. In retalation Poseidon dried up his stream)uhttp://www.theoi.com/Potamos/PotamosInakhos.html  aGreekMythology.com, August 17, 2018b(Inachus was the first king of the city of Argos in Greek mythology. His name was also given to the river 