son, Bob,d1958-  cAmericans2lcdgt  aMassapequa (N.Y.)cUnited States2naf  aStand-up comedyaActing2lcsh  aComediansaActors2lcsh  aMales2lcdgt  aeng  qRobert P.1 aNelson, Robert P.,d1958-  aDo not confuse with: Nelson, Bob, 1956 July 18-  aWikipedia, July 25, 2014b(Bob Nelson (born March 3, 1958), is an American stand-up comedian and actor; born Massapequa, Long Island; Nelson specializes in rubber-faced comedy characters; While he formerly incorporated profanity into his act, Nelson now works clean. Bob had performed and shared at a series of church seminars; and was seen regularly on "Bananas Comedy",  a family-friendly television comedy show. Bob won an Emmy Award in Philadelphia for a children's show he wrote and starred in, called "Double Muppet Hold the Onions" in 1983. He also had supporting roles and starred in films)  aIMDb, August 20, 2018b(Bob Nelson (II); actor, writer; born on March 3, 1958 in Massapequa, Long Island, New York, USA as Robert P. Nelson)00680nz  a2200205n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002100115100002200136370004000158372002200198373003700220374002500257375001600282377000800298670016800306no2018111583DLC20180821073325.0180820n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  ano2018111583  a(OCoLC)oca11510164  aOUbengerdacOU1 aHorton, Robert L.  cUnited StateseColumbus (Ohio)2naf  aAgriculture2lcsh  aOhio State University. Extension  aAgriculturists2lcsh  aMales2lcsh  aeng  aScience fun with dairy foods, 2016, ©2016:bpage 2 of cover (Robert L. Horton, PhD, 4-H specialist, Agri-science, Ohio State University Extension, Columbus, Ohio)00850nz  a2200193n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002300115110003300138368001800171370003500189410003200224670012800256670012000384670015200504no2018111584DLC20180821073326.0180820n| azannaabn          |a ana     c  ano2018111584  a(OCoLC)oca11510165  aWaUbengerdacWaU2 aUlu Camii (Gördes, Turkey)  aMosques2lcsh  cTurkeyeGördes (Turkey)2naf2 aUlu Cami (Gördes, Turkey)  aTony Landreau Collection on Turkish Carpet Weaving and Village Life, 16 August 2018:bslide MISULC0001.jpg (name not given)  aHürışık website, 16 August 2018:bGördes Ulu Camii'nin Yıkılacak Söyentileri Rahatsız etti (Ulu Camii)  aazete gördes website, 16 August 2018:bGördes Halkının Dikkatine, Ulu Cami Külttü̈̈r Varlığı Değilmişı Değilmiş (Ulu Cami)04978nz  a2200517n  no2018111585DLC20180821073326.0180820n| azannaabn          |b aaa     c  ano20181115857 aQ2136212wikidata7 a11878630X2gnd  a(OCoLC)oca11510166  aWaUbengerdacWaU0 aNiobec(Greek mythological character)  cGreek mythological character  dQueen, consort of Amphion, King of Thebes  cGreeceeThebes (Greece)2naf  aQueens2lcdgt  aFemales2lcdgt0 aNiobe,cQueen, consort of Amphion, King of Thebes (Mythological character)0 aΝίοβηc(Greek mythological character)0 aНіёбаc(Greek mythological character)0 aNii︠o︡bac(Greek mythological character)0 aНиобаc(Greek mythological character)0 aNiobac(Greek mythological character)0 aNioboc(Greek mythological character)0 a니오베c(Greek mythological character)0 aניובהc(Greek mythological character)0 aNyobehc(Greek mythological character)0 aNiyobehc(Greek mythological character)0 aニオベーc(Greek mythological character)0 aНіобаc(Greek mythological character)0 a尼俄伯c(Greek mythological character)0 aNieboc(Greek mythological character)  aNon-Latin script references not evaluated.  aSteffani, Agostino. Niobe, Regina di Tebe, 2015.  aMusgrave, Thea. Niobe, ©2005.  aScott, Stephen. Minerva's web ; The tears of Niobe, ℗1990.  aEncyclopædia Britannica online, August 20, 2018b(Niobe, in Greek mythology, the daughter of Tantalus (king of Sipylus in Lydia) and the wife of King Amphion of Thebes)  aThe Oxford classical dictionary, 1996b(Niobe, in mythology, daughter of Tantalus and wife of Amphion of Thebes; symbol of grief)  aEncylopedia mythica, via WWW, August 20, 2018b(Niobe; a daughter of Tantalus by the Pleiad Taygete or the Hyad Dione, or, according to others, a daughter of Pelops and the wife of Zethus or Alalcomeneus, while Parthenius relates quite a different story, for he makes her a daughter of Assaon and the wife of Philottus; according to the common story, which represents her as a daughter of Tantalus, she was the sister of Pelops, and married to Amphion, king of Thebes, by whom she became the mother of six sons and six daughters. Being proud of the number of her children, she deemed herself superior to Leto, who had given birth only to two children. Apollo and Artemis, indignant at such presumption, slew all the children of Niobe; Greek: Νίοβη = Niobē)  aThe tragic myth of Niobe, via greeka.com, viewed August 20, 2018b(Niobe; her father was Tantalus, king of a town above Mount Sipylus in Anatolia, but we do not know exactly who her mother was; when Niobe grew up, she got married to Amphion, king of Thebes)uhttps://www.greeka.com/greece-myths/niobe.htm  aGreekMythology.com, August 20, 2018b(Niobe was a character in Greek mythology, daughter of Tantalus and Dione, although Euryanassa has been named as her mother in a few accounts. Her brothers were Pelops and Broteas.  Niobe was married to Amphion.  They had a total of fourteen children, seven male and seven female, and she made fun of the goddess Leto, who only had the twins Apollo and Artemis. Because of her boasting, she caused the anger of the twin gods who did not like that her mother was being taunted; Apollo killed all of Niobe's sons, while Artemis killed all of her daughters. In some accounts, one of her daughters, Meliboea, was left unharmed. Upon seeing his children, Amphion killed himself; Niobe, in despair, fled and reached Mount Sipylus, where she turned into a rock. The rock became known as the Weeping Rock, as it was believed that the rainwater that fell through the pores of the limestone resembled the tears of Niobe)uhttps://www.greekmythology.com/Myths/Mortals/Niobe/niobe.html  aQueen Niobe in Greek mythology, via Greek legends and myths website, August 20, 2018b(Niobe; Queen of Thebes in Greek mythology; husband was Amphion, a son of Zeus, who had taken the throne, alongside his brother Zethus, from Lycus; Niobe was the daughter of Tantalus and Dione (or perhaps the Pleiad Taygete), making Niobe sisters to Pelops and Broteas)  aWikipedia, August 20, 2018b(Niobe (Greek: Νίοβη = Niobē)) Belarusian page (Ніёба = Nii︠o︡ba) Bulgarian page (Ниоба = Nioba) Czech page (Niobé) Esperanto page (Niobo) French page (Niobé fille de Tantale) Korean page (니오베 = Niobe) Hebrew page (ניובה = Nyobeh( Japanese page (ニオベー = Niobē) Ukrainian page (Ніоба = Nioba) Chinese page (尼俄伯 = Niebo)00545nz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003100115046002100146100004300167380002200210382002100232383001000253670010000263no2018111586DLC20180822073147.0180820n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  ano2018111586  a(OCoLC)oca11510167  aTnNvJMLbengerdacTnNvJML  k1996l19972edtf1 aWuorinen, Charles.tSymphonies,nno. 7  aSymphonies2lcgft0 aorchestra2lcmpt  ano. 7  aWuorinen, C. Symphony seven, 1997:btitle page (Symphony seven : orchestra) caption (1996-1997)00686nz  a2200169n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002100115100002900136374006200165377000800227670005200235670022900287no2018111587DLC20180822073147.0180821n| azannaabn          |n aaa        ano2018111587  a(OCoLC)oca11510168  aNzbengerdacNz1 aWilson, Markc(Musician)  aPianistsaKeyboard playersaOrganistsaHymn writers2lcsh  aeng  aSeeds of faith, 2014:btitle page (Mark Wilson)  aMark Wilson - Rendall music WWW site, viewed on August 15, 2018:bhome (Mark is a Queenstown legend and well-known 