died 1712, perhaps in London; humanist scholar, poet, author of the Dictionarium valachico-latinum; author of the first metric verse in Romanian literature)  aRumanian Studies, Volume 2, 1973:bpage 88 and following (Mihail Halici of Caransebeş (1643-1712); scholar from the Banat; Romanian poet and lexicographer; son of Mihail Halici senior (1615-1671), a juryman in the Caransebeş senate who had attempted a versified translation of the Book of Psalms into Romania; also known as Michael Halicius; Halici Mihály; settled in England and lived there until his death)00681nz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002300115046002700138100003100165370001700196374002200213670006600235670019800301no2018111610DLC20180821073338.0180820n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  ano2018111610  a(OCoLC)oca11510191  aWaUbengerdacWaU  f1888-03-25g19692edtf1 aHasager, Niels,d1888-1969  cDenmark2naf  aJournalists2lcsh  aBladet, 1958:btitle page (Niels Hasager; born 25 March 1888)  aDansk kulturarv, via WWW, Aug. 20, 2018b(Hasager, Niels, 1888-1969, journalist and chief editor)uhttps://www.danskkulturarv.dk/find/Hasager,+Niels+(1888-1969)+journalist+og+chefredakt%C3%B8r/01115nz  a2200205n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002300115046001500138110004400153368002500197370002300222372001500245377000800260670005900268670058200327no2018111611DLC20180821073338.0180820n| azannaabn          |n ana     c  ano2018111611  a(OCoLC)oca11510192  aWaUbengerdacWaU  s19912edtf2 aSunship (Musical group : Great Britain)  aMusical groups2lcsh  cGreat Britain2naf  aJazz2lcsh  aeng  aThe history of acid jazz, 2001:bsong tracks (Sunship)  aSunship website, Oct. 5, 2016:babout (Ceri Evans, Jazz pianist and former member of The Brand New Heavies, launched Sunship in 1991. Releases on the legendary Acid Jazz label followed and from there he became involved with the critically acclaimed Dorado label recording and producing as Sunship (1992). Ceri won a MOBO award as Best Jazz Artist for his self titled album “Sunship” on filter records in 1997. With UK garage retaining such an ardent following, and also picking up new followers realizing the strength of the music, Sunship is busier than ever in the clubs)00913nz  a2200205n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040003400115100004900149370002600198372005500224373003100279374004900310377000800359670006900367670027100436no2018111612DLC20180823073249.0180820n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  ano2018111612  a(OCoLC)oca11510193  aNjEiCbengerdacNjEiCdNjEiC1 aBailey, Genevievec(Motion picture director)  eMelbourne (Vic.)2naf  aShort filmsaDocumentary filmsaMusic videos2lcsh  aProud Mother Pictures2naf  aMotion picture producers and directors2lcsh  aeng  aI am eleven, 2012:bopening credits (a film by Genevieve Bailey)  ahttp://www.iameleven.com/ website, August 8, 2018bAbout page (Genevieve Bailey; director, producer, cinematographer, editor; has produced over 40 short films, documentaries and music videos; grew up in Melbourne; founder of Proud Mother Pictures production company)04181nz  a2200289n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002300115046003300138100003200171370007200203372003000275374005700305375001700362377000800379378001400387400003400401400003700435670007200472670012800544670137700672670085202049670099002901no2018111613DLC20180821073338.0180820n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  ano2018111613  a(OCoLC)oca11510194  aWaUbengerdacWaU  f1939-05-31g2005-08-112edtf1 aRobinson, Billy,d1939-2005  aFort Worth (Tex.)bOttawa (Ont.)cUnited StateseOttawa (Ont.)2naf  aJazzaMusic therapy2lcsh  aJazz musiciansaSaxophonistsaMusic therapists2lcsh  aMales2lcdgt  aeng  qBilly Tom1 aSamad, Jathiya A.,d1939-20051 aSamad, Jathiya Abdus,d1939-2005  aEl jazz canadiense, between 1970 and 1979?:blabel (Billy Robinson)  aOCLC, October 4, 2016b(access points: Robinson, Billy; Samad, Jathiya A., 1939- ; usage: Billy Robinson; Jathiya A. Samad)  aJazzhouse website, 2005, viewed online October 4, 2016:bthe last post (Billy Robinson, tenor saxophone. Saxophonist, composer, bandleader and educator Billy Robinson died in Ottawa on August 11, 2005, of a heart attack. Billy was born in Fort Worth, Texas on May 31, 1939. A big-toned tenor in the great Texas tradition. Billy lived during the mid-'60s in San Francisco, and then moved to New York, where he worked with Charles Mingus 1969-70, but unfortunately never recorded with him. Billy converted to Islam in the late '60s, and was a practising Muslim for the rest of his life. He moved to Montreal when he married his first wife in 1970, but he was always ready to return to the United States for interesting gigs with his many musical friends. Their son Zurvan was born in 1970. In 1974, he married Suzanne Cyr and in 1978 they moved to Ottawa. Again, apart from the occasional, highly anticipated, local gig most of his work took him back to the U.S. Billy's recording debut was in January 1972, on Archie Shepp's Attica Blues (Impulse!), and in September of that year he recorded Evolution's Blend for Radio Canada International. In 1973 he appeared on pianist Sadik Hakim's Grey Cup Caper. In 1991 he recorded Doubt Dropping (Tocma), followed by A Short Turn to the Future (Tocma) in 1998. In April 2005, Billy recorded a new CD to be issued later in the year)  aOttawa citizen, July 20, 1997, viewed online Oct. 4, 2016:b(Billy Tom Williams; Billy Robinson's life follows the familiar route of contemporary American black life, from the civil rights movement to San Francisco's Summer of Love to the discovery of Islam amid the New York jazz scene of Charles Mingus and heroin, along the way it veers onto a path of holistic jazz healing. Billy's Muslim name is Jathiya Abdus Samad. Billy splits his time between practising in Ottawa, recording his music and pursuing his healing in The City -- New York -- with a holistic physician called "Dr. Ali."  He is also working on a book about his music therapy, which combines musical theories, Islamic beliefs and some of the University of Ottawa psychology courses he took a few years back. He has used his time in Canada to study music, psychology and religion)  aOttawa citizen, August 16, 2005, viewed online October 4, 2016:b"Music scientist" a player and teacher (musician, composer and bandleader, and beloved as a warm, spiritual man, tenor saxophonist Billy Robinson once said he considered himself a "music scientist." Texas-born Robinson, a longtime resident of Ottawa died last Thursday [August 11, 2005] in Ottawa of a heart attack. He converted in the late 1960s, taking the Muslim name Jathiya Abdus Samad. By 1978, Robinson was living in Ottawa. Such interests led Robinson several years ago to study and practise music therapy. Blending his studies in psychology at the University of Ottawa with his religious beliefs, Robinson composed musical pieces to accompany his therapeutic treatments, some of which he carried out in New York City in collaboration with a holistic doctor. Robinson was writing a book on the subject, Audioneural Musiciatry, along with another publication, Jazz Improvisation and the Art of Self, when he died)00634nz  a2200169n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002300115046003300138100003700171378002100208670011900229670011600348no2018111614DLC20180821073339.0180820n| azannaabn          |n aaa     c  ano2018111614  a(OCoLC)oca11510195  aOkUbengerdacOkU  f1909-07-28g1983-08-292edtf1 aHartley, Margaret L.,d1909-1983  qMargaret Lohlker  aThe Southwest review reader, 1974:btitle page (edited by Margaret L. Hartley) page vii (Margaret Lohlker Hartley)  aSouthwest review, v