Disembodied Poetics at Naropa University, counting Allen Ginsberg and William Burroughs as “poetic” godfathers. He graduated from The University of California, Santa Cruz with a degree in music/sociology and was certified at the music /production program at the Pyramind Institute in San Francisco.)  aOCLC, August 3, 2018:b(access point: Bye, Ambrose; usage: Ambrose Bye)  aDrunken Boat #16, viewed online August 22, 2018:bAmbrose Bye (Ambrose Bye, composer/musician/producer ... and was trained as an audio engineer at the music /production program at Pyramind in San Francisco.)00922nz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002100115100002100136370004600157374002100203375000900224400001400233670049300247no2018113379DLC20180823073124.0180822n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  ano2018113379  a(OCoLC)oca11514630  aNNbengerdacNN1 aAethelman, G. C.  aSantander (Spain)fSalamanca (Spain)2naf  aHistorians2lcsh  amale1 aAethelman  aAethelman, G. C. Atlántida, 2017:btitle page (G.C. Aethelman) front cover flap (Aethelman; historian; born in Santander, Spain; got his degree and took his courses for his doctorate in Salamanca, Spain; director of the Editorial Akergori where he published since 2012 his magazine Atlántida centered on the Bronze Age; also published Breves apuntes sobre el significado de las pinturas prehistóricas de Cantabria, Lanzarote y el Grial en el Reino de Cantabria and Carta a Cipriano)00642nz  a2200181n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002900115100006900144380001900213382003000232384001200262400006800274670011800342no2018113380DLC20180823073124.0180822n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  ano2018113380  a(OCoLC)oca11514631  aUPB-MubengerdacUPB-Mu1 aPasqualino, Peter,d-1766.tSonatas,mcello, continuo.,rA major  aSonatas2lcgft0 acellon1acontinuo2lcmpt0 aA major1 aPasqualino, Peter,d-1766.tSonate,mcello, continuo.,rA major  aPasqualino, Peter. Sonate, [between 1900 and 1989]bcaption (Sonate von Pasqualino berabeitet von Carl Schroeder)03907nz  a2200301n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002500115046002100140110005600161368004900217370003200266372008000298510005300378510005600431510007300487510007100560670012600631670099300757670048201750670057802232670030002810670014703110670034803257no2018113381DLC20180823073124.0180822n| azannaabn          |a ana     c  ano2018113381  a(OCoLC)oca11514632  aABAUbengerdacABAU  s1901t19602edtf2 aUniversity of Toronto.bFaculty of Arts (1901-1960)  aUniversities and colleges--Departments2lcsh  cCanadaeToronto (Ont.)2naf  aEducation, HumanisticaScience--Study and teaching (Higher)aResearch2lcsh2 wriHierarchical superior:aUniversity of Toronto2 wriPredecessor:aUniversity College (Toronto, Ont.)2 wriPredecessor:aUniversity of Toronto.bFaculty of Arts (1850-1853)2 wriSuccessor:aUniversity of Toronto.bFaculty of Arts and Science  aDrugs and the cell cycle, 1973:btitle page ( ... A. M. Zimmermann, Department of Zoology, University of Toronto, Canada)  aBrown, R.C. Arts and Science at Toronto, ©2013:bpage 22 (The Faculty of Arts was in limbo in 1887. ... Tiny King's College, limping along with a handful of students in the 1840s, had had a dean and a Faculty of Arts. So too did the University of Toronto in 1850. But the 1853 University Act swept the faculty, de jure, away, putting University College in its place. ... But though not recognized in law or as an academic unit within the university, "Arts" existed de facto and was recognized as such throughout the university and University College) pages 32-33 (While the  [Province of Ontario] government was drafting the new 1901 [University] act, Richard Harcourt, Minister of Education, asked [University Council president] Loudon about sentiment in the university to renewing legal recognition of the Faculty of Arts and a dean of arts. .. Then, in brief compass, the new act provided for appointment by the lieutenant governor of a president of the university and a dean of arts)  aBiography -- Wright, Robert Ramsay (Volume XVI 1931-1940) -- Dictionary of Canadian biography, via WWW, June 22, 2018:b( ... Ramsay Wright held a number of posts at the University of Toronto. ... He would serve under deans William Thomas Aikins, Uzziel Ogden, Richard Andrew Reeve, and Charles Kirk Clarke and see the opening of the medical building in 1903. Wright also became dean of the faculty of arts in 1901)uhttp://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/wright_robert_ramsay_16E.html  aOntario. Department of Education. Report of the Minister of Education, Province of Ontario, for the year 1904, 1905:bpage 272 (Addendum A. --- Report of the Dean of the Faculty of Arts. University of Toronto, March 22nd, 1905. [To] James Loudon, Esq., LL.D., President of the University of Toronto. Sir: I beg to submit herewith a detailed list, for the academic year 1903-04, of the personnel of the teaching staff of  the Faculty of Arts of the University of Toronto ... ) page 277 ( ... I am, Sir, Yours faithfully, (Sgd.) R. Ramsay Wright, Dean of the Faculty of Arts)  aInternational Mathematical Congress (1924 : Toronto, Ont.). Proceedings of the International Mathematical Congress held in Toronto, August 11-16, 1924, vol. 1, 1928:bpage 33 (Delegates and members ... DeLury, Professor A. T., Dean of the Faculty of Arts, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada)  aUniversity of Toronto quarterly, July 1957:bpage 482 (Contributors ... M. St.A. Woodside, Dean of the Faculty of Arts, University of Toronto)  aAbout the University and the Faculty - Faculty of Arts & Science Calendar, via WWW, Aug. 22, 2018:b(University of Toronto. Faculty of Arts & Science calendar ... History of the University and the Faculty ... 1960: Name of Faculty of Arts changed to "Faculty of Arts and Science")uhttps:/