  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002500115046002100140110005600161368004900217370003200266372008000298510005300378510008400431510005400515510007100569670061900640670099301259670048202252no2018113401DLC20180823073126.0180822n| azannaabn          |a ana     c  ano2018113401  a(OCoLC)oca11514652  aABAUbengerdacABAU  s1850t18532edtf2 aUniversity of Toronto.bFaculty of Arts (1850-1853)  aUniversities and colleges--Departments2lcsh  cCanadaeToronto (Ont.)2naf  aEducation, HumanisticaScience--Study and teaching (Higher)aResearch2lcsh2 wriHierarchical superior:aUniversity of Toronto2 wriPredecessor:aUniversity of King's College (Toronto, Ont.).bFaculty of Arts2 wriSuccessor:aUniversity College (Toronto, Ont.)2 wriSuccessor:aUniversity of Toronto.bFaculty of Arts (1901-1960)  aBrown, R.C. Arts and Science at Toronto, ©2013:bpreface (The Faculty of Arts and Science -- known as the Faculty of Arts until 1960 -- is older than the University of Toronto. its origin is found in the first provincial university in Ontario, King's College, chartered in 1827 and opened in June 1843 ... .  [I]n January 1850, the secular University of Toronto became the provincial university, succeeding King's College) page 8 (The University Act of 1849 gave the University of Toronto three faculties, Arts, Law, and Medicine, and transferred the professors of the former King's College to the new university)  aBrown, R.C. Arts and Science at Toronto, ©2013:bpage 22 (The Faculty of Arts was in limbo in 1887. ... Tiny King's College, limping along with a handful of students in the 1840s, had had a dean and a Faculty of Arts. So too did the University of Toronto in 1850. But the 1853 University Act swept the faculty, de jure, away, putting University College in its place. ... But though not recognized in law or as an academic unit within the university, "Arts" existed de facto and was recognized as such throughout the university and University College) pages 32-33 (While the  [Province of Ontario] government was drafting the new 1901 [University] act, Richard Harcourt, Minister of Education, asked [University Council president] Loudon about sentiment in the university to renewing legal recognition of the Faculty of Arts and a dean of arts. .. Then, in brief compass, the new act provided for appointment by the lieutenant governor of a president of the university and a dean of arts)  aBiography -- Wright, Robert Ramsay (Volume XVI 1931-1940) -- Dictionary of Canadian biography, via WWW, June 22, 2018:b( ... Ramsay Wright held a number of posts at the University of Toronto. ... He would serve under deans William Thomas Aikins, Uzziel Ogden, Richard Andrew Reeve, and Charles Kirk Clarke and see the opening of the medical building in 1903. Wright also became dean of the faculty of arts in 1901)uhttp://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/wright_robert_ramsay_16E.html00555nz  a2200169n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002700115100004700142377000800189381001400197400003800211670013600249no2018113402DLC20180823073126.0180822n| azannaabn          |a aaa     c  ano2018113402  a(OCoLC)oca11514653  aICrlFbengerdacICrlF1 aMurray, Julie,d1969-tPresident.lSpanish  aspa  aFrumholtz1 aMurray, Julie,d1969-tPresidente  aMurray, Julie. Presidente, 2019:btitle page verso (Spanish translators, Telma Frumholtz, Maria Puchol ; original title, President)01050nz  a2200217n  4500001001300000003000400013005001700017008004100034010001700075035002300092040002700115100008400142377000800226381000700234400008600241400006600327400008000393667004700473670014100520670017100661no2018113403DLC20180823073126.0180822n| azannaabn          |a aab     c  ano2018113403  a(OCoLC)oca11514654  aICrlFbengerdacICrlF1 aArden, Paul.tIt's not how good you are, it's how good you want to be.lChinese  achi  aXu1 aArden, Paul.tI